====== Leetcode 435. Non-overlapping Intervals =======
Step-by-step algorithm for erasing minimum overlapping intervals:
Step 1: If the number of intervals is 0, return 0.
Step 2: Sort all intervals based on their ending time in ascending order.
Step 3: Initialize a variable cot to 0.
This will count how many intervals need to be removed.
Step 4: Store the ending time of the first interval in a variable called end.
Step 5: Traverse the intervals from the second interval to the last.
•	Sub-step 5a: Check if the current interval’s start time is less than end.
o	If yes (overlap exists):
	Increase cot by 1.
	Do not update end because we keep the interval with the smaller end time (already chosen).
o	If no (no overlap):
	Update end to the ending time of the current interval.
	This means we select this interval.
Step 6: After checking all intervals, return cot.

Time Complexity:
•	Sorting takes O(n log n)
•	Single traversal takes O(n)
Total Time Complexity = O(n log n)

Space Complexity:
•	Only a few variables are used.
Space Complexity = O(1)




   -----------------> Code <-------------
class Solution {
    public int eraseOverlapIntervals(int[][] intervals) {
        if(intervals.length==0)return 0;

        Arrays.sort(intervals, (a,b)-> Integer.compare(a[1],b[1]));
        int cot=0;
        int n=intervals.length;
        int end=intervals[0][1];
        
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            if(intervals[i][0]<end){
                cot++;
            }else{
                end=intervals[i][1];
            }
        }
        return cot;
    }
}
